          <TD colSpan=3D2><SPAN class=3Ddesc=20
            id=3Dctl00_ctl00_ctl00_content_content_content_ITEM_DESC>If =
you've=20
            been reading the Webservice articles on this site in the =
order that=20
            they are listed, then this is your first Webservice. As =
WebServices=20
            go, this is not the simplest of WebServices, but my thinking =
was=20
            that I could take you through the paces of building a =
Webservice=20
            that addresses some of things you'll need to know when =
building=20
            "real world" WebServices while at the same time not =
overwhelm you=20
            with all these new things. If you haven't read the previous=20
            articles, I strongly suggest you do. This tutorial assumes =
you have=20
            read those articles or know about the things that were =
discussed=20
            there.=20
            <P>In this tutorial, we'll start with building a WebService =
client=20
            for an existing WebService. Then we'll build the WebService =
itself.=20
            This way, you don't have to get both projects up and running =
before=20
            you can have any fun at all. </P>
            <H2>Building the WebService Client</H2>
            <P>So lets start by building a WebService client for an =
existing=20
            Webservice. Just to get our feet wet as it were. If you go =
to <A=20
            title=3Dxmethods =
href=3D"http://www.xmethods.com/ve2/index.po"=20
            rel=3Dnofollow>Xmethods.com</A> you'll see there are a =
number of=20
            WebServices people have posted links for. We'll use one of =
these=20
            services for starters. </P>
            <P>You might notice that I'm being partial here. I've chosen =
the <A=20
            title=3D"Send an Email WebService"=20
            href=3D"http://www.xmethods.com/ve2/ViewListing.po?" =
rel=3Dnofollow=20
            key=3D"uuid:E7B84967-6183-4746-2B3D-E04259314FF5">Send an=20
            Email</A>&nbsp;WebService. We'll write a simple client for =
this=20
            WebService. Before we can begin to use any WebService, we =
need to=20
            have the WSDL file. With Delphi, you simply need the URL to =
a WSDL=20
            file. You could use a physical WSDL file as well, but the =
Expert=20
            available to us, allows us to supply the URL to a WSDL file =
so we'll=20
            use this capability. The expert will generate the Interface =
files=20
            for us. </P>
            <H2>WSDL File</H2>
            <P>Let's take a brief look at the WSDL file. The URL for the =
WSDL=20
            file is <A title=3DIemailService=20
            =
href=3D"http://webservices.matlus.com/scripts/emailwebservice.dll/wsdl/Ie=
mailService"=20
            =
rel=3Dnofollow>http://webservices.matlus.com/scripts/emailwebservice.dll/=
wsdl/IemailService</A>.=20
            If you're using I.E, you can click on this link to see the =
WSDL file=20
            inside your browser. Without going into too many details =
(the WSDL=20
            spec. can be quite complex for the faint hearted), lets take =
a look=20
            at some of the elements you should be aware of. </P>
            <P>
            <OL>
              <LI>The <B>&lt;message&gt;</B> element. The =
<CODE>name</CODE>=20
              attribute contains the name of the method with the word=20
              <CODE>Request</CODE> added to it. This is part of the =
spec. This=20
              is not the element that really tells you the name of the =
method=20
              however. That comes later=20
              <LI>the <B>&lt;part&gt;</B> element's <CODE>name</CODE> =
attribute=20
              tells you the name of the parameters while the =
<CODE>type</CODE>=20
              attribute tells you the type of the parameters.=20
              <LI>Next, take a look at the next <B>&lt;message&gt; =
</B>element.=20
              The <CODE>name</CODE> attribute has the name of the method =
with=20
              the word <CODE>Response</CODE> at the end of it. Once =
again, this=20
              is as per spec.=20
              <LI>Under this element the <B>&lt;part&gt; </B>element's=20
              <CODE>name</CODE> attribute tells you what is being =
returned and=20
              the <CODE>type</CODE> attribute tells you the type of this =
return=20
              value.=20
              <LI>The next important element is the <B>&lt;operation&gt; =

              </B>element. This is the element that really gives you the =
remote=20
              method name. In this case <CODE>SendMail</CODE>.=20
              <LI>The <B>&lt;soap:operation&gt; </B>element has an =
attribute=20
              called <CODE>soapAction</CODE>. You'll see this mentioned =
once=20
              again later in this tutorial. The <CODE>value</CODE> of =
this=20
              attribute is generated for you automatically by the Delphi =
6 SOAP=20
              framework. In particular by the =
<CODE>TWSDLHTMLPublish</CODE>=20
              component at the server end. This is the component that =
actually=20
              generated this WSDL file in the first place.(if you =
examine the=20
              URL for this WSDL file, you'll notice that it points to an =
action=20
              in the ISAPI.)=20
              <LI>The <B>&lt;service&gt;</B> element and it's =
<CODE>name</CODE>=20
              attribute are the next elements to look at. We'll see =
these again=20
              when building the client later. In particular, when =
populating the=20
              <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component's <CODE>Service</CODE> and =

              <CODE>Port</CODE> properties. </LI></OL>
            <P>That's probably some of the more important things to look =
for in=20
            a WSDL file when building a Webservice client. Of course, =
things can=20
            get a bit funky when you run into problems with namespaces =
etc. when=20
            dealing with services that were either built according to an =
older=20
            or newer SOAP specification, or different development tool. =
These=20
            are some of the issues you're going to have to live with =
when living=20
            on the bleeding edge of technology. </P>
            <P>I'd also like to take this opportunity to highlight =
another=20
            aspect that relates to XML. Now you don't need to get too =
involved=20
            with this at this point in time. It'll all come together =
soon=20
            enough. So if you don't understand it now, it's ok. For the =
moment=20
            there is no real need to <B>have</B> to understand it. I'm =
talking=20
            about <B>Namespaces</B>. Any good book on XML will give you =
a more=20
            detailed explanation. However, I'll explain things as they =
relate to=20
            SOAP and in particular the WSDL file in question. Right at =
the start=20
            of this WSDL file, you'll see some namespaces being =
declared. In=20
            I.E. they will be highlighted in red (You must have wondered =
about=20
            this when you first show the WSDL file right?). Namespaces =
are=20
            declared as attributes of an element. In this case the=20
            <B>&lt;definitions&gt;</B> element =
like</P><PRE><CODE>xmlns:soap=3D"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/wsdl/soap/" =
</CODE></PRE>
            <P>
            <P>This declaration now gives us a namespace called=20
            <B><CODE>soap</CODE></B>. Further down in the WSDL file =
there are a=20
            few elements that start with the word <CODE>soap</CODE>. For =

            example, <B>&lt;soap:operation&gt;</B>. This is really the=20
            <B>&lt;operation&gt;</B> element in the =
<B><CODE>soap</CODE></B>=20
            namespace. You can tell this by the virtue of the fact that =
there is=20
            a <B>:</B> (colon) between the word <CODE>soap</CODE> and=20
            <CODE>operation</CODE>. Hope that makes some sense at this =
point.=20
            </P>
            <H2>WebService Client Project</H2>
            <OL>
              <LI>Start a new project=20
              <LI>Then <CODE>File | New | Other</CODE>=20
              <LI>In the New Items dialog, choose the =
<CODE>WebServices</CODE>=20
              tab=20
              <LI>Choose the <CODE>WebServices Importer</CODE>=20
              <LI>Click OK </LI></OL>
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService1.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG src=3D"" border=3D0></A> =
<BR><B>Figure=20
            1: Selecting the WebServices Importer</B> </P>
            <P>This should show you the Wizard. </P>
