            <P>From the code listing above, you should see that the =
interface is=20
            called <CODE>IEmailService</CODE> and that is has one method =
(a=20
            function in this case) called <CODE>SendMail</CODE>. This =
method,=20
            expects to see the parameters required to send an email. =
What is=20
            interesting to note is that this <CODE>Interface</CODE> is =
derived=20
            from an interface called <CODE>IInvokable</CODE>. This is =
new to=20
            Delphi 6. <CODE>IInvokable</CODE> is almost exactly the same =
as=20
            <CODE>IInterface</CODE> (or <CODE>IUnknown</CODE>) which is =
the base=20
            type for all Delphi Interfaces. The key difference is that =
it is=20
            compiled with Run time Type information using the <CODE>{$M =
}</CODE>=20
            compiler directive. I mentioned in the previous articles, =
the Delphi=20
            SOAP implementation uses RTTI and Interfaces. This is one =
part of=20
            that equation. </P>
            <P>The next thing to note is the <CODE>initialization</CODE> =
section=20
            of the unit that was generated. The invocation registry is =
again new=20
            to Delphi 6. The Delphi 6 SOAP framework uses this =
"registry" to be=20
            able to automatically instantiate instances of Invokable =
classes=20
            (classes that implement interfaces derived from IInvokable) =
and=20
            Remoteable classes (we'll get to these in a later tutorial). =

            Needless to say, that if you don't register things that need =
to be=20
            registered, your WebServices will not work. The Online help =
has a=20
            decent explanation of the <CODE>RegisterInterface</CODE> =
method and=20
            the parameters expected (most of them are optional). Also =
notice the=20
            <CODE>stdcall</CODE> directive. Currently, the Delphi =
default=20
            calling convention (register) is not yet supported. You =
could use=20
            any other calling convention that Delphi supports, such as=20
            <CODE>cdecl, pascal, or safecall</CODE>. It is not =
recommended that=20
            you use <CODE>safecall</CODE> since <CODE>safecall</CODE> =
calls are=20
            wrapped with an exception handler by the compiler and this =
may=20
            swallow more exceptions than you'd want to. The default is =
to use=20
            <CODE>stdcall</CODE> so I suggest you stick with this.</P>
            <P>Lets switch to the main form of the application and drop =
down a=20
            <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component on the form. You'll find =
this=20
            component in the WebServices palette. This component has 4 =
key=20
            properties. </P>
            <OL>
              <LI><CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE>=20
              <LI><CODE>Service</CODE>=20
              <LI><CODE>Port</CODE>=20
              <LI><CODE>URL</CODE> </LI></OL>
            <P>You either use the <CODE>WSDLLocation, Service and =
Port</CODE>=20
            properties or just the <CODE>URL</CODE> property. </P>
            <P>
            <P>Given a WSDLLocation (URL) the <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> =
component is=20
            capable of parsing out the WSDL file and populating the=20
            <CODE>Service</CODE> and <CODE>Port</CODE> property dropdown =
lists.=20
            So lets fill in the WSDLLocation with the same URL we used =
earlier.=20
            Now drop down the <CODE>Service</CODE> property's combo box =
and=20
            select <CODE>IEmailServiceservice</CODE>. Do the same for =
the=20
            <CODE>Port</CODE> property and select=20
            <CODE>IEmailServicePort</CODE>. </P>
