            <P>
            <P>Basically, you really need just one line of code. Most of =
the=20
            code you see in the OnClick event is really related to GUI =
frills.=20
            The one line of code that really does the work is: </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE></CODE><PRE><FONT =
face=3D"Courier New">    <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>if</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>HTTPRIO1</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#0000ff><B>as</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>IEmailService</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>SendMail</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>txtToAddress</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>Text</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>txtFromAddress</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Text</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT>
      <FONT color=3D#000080>txtSubject</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>Text</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>MemMessageBody</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Lines</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Text</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>&lt;&gt;</FONT> <FONT color=3D#800080>0</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#0000ff><B>then</B></FONT></FONT>
</PRE></DIV></DIV><PRE></PRE>
            <P>If you run this application and fill in the values to =
send=20
            yourself an email and hit the send button, you'll receive =
and email=20
            if all goes well.</P>
            <H2>Behind the Scenes</H2>So what's happening? First of all, =
you're=20
            using a WebService that is already there for you to use. =
Because the=20
            service exists, you can use it without the need to build it=20
            yourself. This is one the reasons that SOAP is so exciting. =
SOAP has=20
            added another dimension to the word "re-usability".=20
            <P>Behind the scenes, the <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component =
has built=20
            a SOAP envelope for you and sent it over to the WebService =
server.=20
            The server knows what to do with this SOAP envelop. This =
SOAP=20
            envelope (for those interested) looks like this: </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE></CODE><PRE>POST =
http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/emailwebservice.dll/soap/IEmailservice =
HTTP/1.0<BR>Accept: application/octet-stream, text/xml<BR>SOAPAction: =
"urn:EmailIPortTypeInft-IEmailService"<BR>Content-Type: =
text/xml<BR>User-Agent: Borland SOAP 1.1<BR>Host: =
matlus.matlus.com<BR>Content-Length: 759<BR>Proxy-Connection: =
Keep-Alive<BR>Pragma: no-cache<BR><BR>&lt;?xml version=3D"1.0" =
encoding=3D'UTF-8'?&gt;<BR>  &lt;SOAP-ENV:Envelope =
xmlns:SOAP-ENV=3D"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/"<BR>    =
xmlns:xsd=3D"http://www.w3.org/1999/XMLSchema"<BR>    =
xmlns:xsi=3D"http://www.w3.org/1999/XMLSchema-instance"<BR>    =
xmlns:SOAP-ENC=3D"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/encoding/"&gt; <BR>    =
&lt;SOAP-ENV:Body&gt; <BR>      &lt;NS1:SendMail =
xmlns:NS1=3D"urn:EmailIPortTypeInft-IEmailService" =
SOAP-ENV:encodingStyle=3D"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/encoding/"&gt; =
<BR>        &lt;NS1:ToAddress =
xsi:type=3D"xsd:string"&gt;shivk@erols.com&lt;/NS1:ToAddress&gt; <BR>    =
    &lt;NS1:FromAddress xsi:type=3D"xsd:string"&gt;Shiv =
Kumar&lt;shiv@matlus.com&gt;&lt;/NS1:FromAddress&gt; <BR>        =
&lt;NS1:ASubject xsi:type=3D"xsd:string"&gt; =
Testing&lt;/NS1:ASubject&gt; <BR>        &lt;NS1:MsgBody =
xsi:type=3D"xsd:string"&gt;This is a TEST MESSAGE&lt;/NS1:MsgBody&gt; =
<BR>      &lt;/NS1:SendMail&gt; <BR>    &lt;/SOAP-ENV:Body&gt; <BR>  =
&lt;/SOAP-ENV:Envelope&gt;<BR></PRE></DIV></DIV>Let=20
            me highlight some of the things you might want/need to know. =
<BR>
            <OL>
              <LI>In the HTTP header take a look at the URL that was =
constructed=20
              -=20
              =
<CODE>http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/emailwebservice.dll/soap/Iemailser=
vice</CODE>.=20
              What is key here is the part after the=20
              <CODE>emailwebservice.dll</CODE>. On the server side, when =
the=20
              Service receives this Request a component called=20
              <CODE>THTTPSOAPDispatcher</CODE> fields this request =
(because it=20
              has PathInfo <CODE>/soap</CODE>). It then hands it over to =
another=20
              component called <CODE>THTTPPascalInvoker</CODE>. This =
component=20
              looks up the Invocation Registry and tries to match the =
name of=20
              the interface (<CODE>IEmailService</CODE>). If it finds a =
match,=20
              it instantiates an instance of the class that implements =
this=20
              interface. We'll get to this when we build the server =
side. But=20
              you should know, that this is done for you automatically =
by the=20
              Delphi 6 SOAP framework.=20
              <LI>The <CODE>SOAPAction</CODE> HTTP header. This is a new =
HTTP=20
              header required for SOAP calls. This is one of the headers =
that a=20
              firewall/proxy can look for to either allow/disallow SOAP =
calls=20
              past it for security reasons. Of course, the SOAP spec. =
does not=20
              make this mandatory. I would guess that in time this =
header will=20
              be mandatory.=20
              <LI>Next, within the XML Document (SOAP envelope) you =
should see=20
              the name of the method.=20
              <LI>You should also see the parameters and values to these =

              parameters that are being sent to the server. </LI></OL>
            <P>The server processed this packet and responded with =
another SOAP=20
            packet that looks like this </P><CODE></CODE>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><PRE>HTTP/1.1 200 OK<BR>Server: =
Microsoft-IIS/5.0<BR>Date: Sat, 14 Jul 2001 07:35:44 =
GMT<BR>Content-Type: text/xml<BR>Content-Length: =
531<BR>Content:<BR><BR>&lt;?xml version=3D"1.0" =
encoding=3D'UTF-8'?&gt;<BR>  &lt;SOAP-ENV:Envelope =
xmlns:SOAP-ENV=3D"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/"<BR>    =
xmlns:xsd=3D"http://www.w3.org/1999/XMLSchema"<BR>    =
xmlns:xsi=3D"http://www.w3.org/1999/XMLSchema-instance"<BR>    =
xmlns:SOAP-ENC=3Dhttp://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/encoding/&gt;<BR>    =
&lt;SOAP-ENV:Body&gt;<BR>      &lt;NS1:SendMailResponse =
xmlns:NS1=3D"urn:EmailIPortTypeInft-IEmailService" =
SOAP-ENV:encodingStyle=3Dhttp://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/encoding/&gt;<BR=
>        &lt;NS1:return xsi:type=3D"xsd:int"&gt;0&lt;/NS1:return&gt;<BR> =
     &lt;/NS1:SendMailResponse&gt;<BR>    &lt;/SOAP-ENV:Body&gt;<BR>  =
&lt;/SOAP-ENV:Envelope&gt;<BR></PRE></DIV></DIV>After=20
            looking at all of this, I'm sure you'll appreciate the fact =
that the=20
            Delphi 6 SOAP framework hides the HTTP and XML details from =
you and=20
            does all of the hard work while you wrote a simple line of =
code!=20
            <P>If you really want to know what all the XML stuff is =
you'll need=20
            to go deeper into the SOAP spec and XML. If you want to be a =
good=20
            SOAP programmer you can't avoid this. For now however, we =
don't=20
            <B>need </B>to know. </P>
            <P>What if this WebService was written in Java or C ? How =
would they=20
            know what to do with such as request? In particular, how do =
they=20
            treat <CODE>IEmailService</CODE>? As I mentioned in the =
previous=20
            articles, Delphi 6's SOAP implementation uses Interfaces. =
Other SOAP=20
            tool kits don't have to. So they will understand this =
request the=20
            way <B>They</B> need to. SOAP is an implementation and =
platform=20
            agnostic protocol. With WebServices it doesn't matter what =
tool the=20
            service is written in or what platform its running on. Well, =
this is=20
            the ideal scenario. Currently, there are interoperability =
issues,=20
            but we'll get there one day I'm sure. For the most part, =
things=20
            should work just fine. </P>
            <P>With the knowledge you've gained so far, you should be =
able to=20
            experiment with other WebServices listed on the XMethods web =
site. I=20
            suggest you do this and then come back here to learn how to =
build=20
            the server for this WebService. </P>
            <H2>THTTPRIO Explained</H2>When building a WebService =
client, the=20
            <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component is the key component. =
Therefore, I'd=20
            like to explain some of the properties of this component, =
while=20
            we're still playing with the client side. Take a look at =
<B>Figure=20
            5</B>=20
            <P>
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService5.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG height=3D125 hspace=3D0 =
src=3D"" width=3D78=20
            border=3D0></A> <BR><B>Figure 5: Showing the Properties of=20
            THTTPRIO</B> </P>
            <P>We've used the <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> property, the=20
            <CODE>Service</CODE> property and the <CODE>Port</CODE>. =
Keep in=20
            mind, that for you to be able to use the =
<CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE>=20
            in design time (or run time) you need access to the Internet =
if your=20
            <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> is a URL. Also, you use the=20
            <CODE>Service</CODE> and <CODE>Port</CODE> properties only =
when=20
            using the <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> property. Instead of =
using the=20
            <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> property, you could use the=20
            <CODE>URL</CODE> property. When using the <CODE>URL</CODE> =
property,=20
            you don't need (and shouldn't) use the <CODE>Service</CODE> =
and=20
            <CODE>Port</CODE> properties. The value of the =
<CODE>URL</CODE>=20
            property will not be the same as the =
<CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE>. You=20
            need to use the proper value in this case. This is the =
<B><CODE>SOAP=20
            Endpoint URL</CODE></B>. When looking at SOAP services that =
listed=20
            at sites such as XMethods or SALCentral, you will see the =
<CODE>SOAP=20
            Endpoint URL</CODE> listed along with the WSDL file. The=20
            <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component gets the WSDL file at run =
time,=20
            parses it out and finds the <CODE>SOAP Endpoint URL</CODE> =
in the=20
            WSDL File. So it goes without saying, that using the=20
            <CODE>URL</CODE> property will be slightly faster than using =
the=20
            <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> property since some round trips =
are saved.=20
            Keep this in mind, if you are making repeated calls to a =
certain=20
            WebService in your applications. Another interesting point =
to note=20
            is that the URL for the WSDL file and the SOAP Endpoint =
(only for=20
            Delphi 6 build WebServices) have a very small difference. =
The word=20
            <CODE>wsdl</CODE> is replaced with the word =
<CODE>soap</CODE>. Note=20
            that this is by default and that you have the option to =
change this.=20
            </P>
            <P>What if you are behind a proxy/firewall either at home or =
work?=20
            Well, then you're going to have some issues that you'll need =
to=20
            circumvent. You won't be able to use the <B>WebService =
Importer</B>=20
            as I explained earlier. What you'll have to do is use your =
browser=20
            to get to the WSDL File. Save it locally, and then use this =
file=20
            (you can use the Browse button) to fill in the=20
            <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> field in the wizard. Once you've =
done this=20
            and the interface file(s) is created for you, your next =
issue will=20
            be when you attempt to fill in the properties of the=20
            <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component. First, if you look at the=20
            properties shown in <B>Figure 5</B>, you'll see that the=20
            <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component has a <CODE>Proxy</CODE> =
property as=20
            well as <CODE>UserName</CODE> and <CODE>Password</CODE> =
properties.=20
            You'll need to get the details from your Network =
administrator if=20
            you don't know what to fill in here. As an example, if you =
have a=20
            proxy server called <CODE>MyProxy</CODE> that uses port =
8080, then=20
            the <CODE>Proxy</CODE> property needs to be filled in as=20
            <CODE>MyProxy:8080</CODE>. As explained earlier, I suggest =
you use=20
            the <CODE>URL</CODE> property instead of the=20
            <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE>. If you do plan to use the=20
            <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> property, you need to use the file =
path=20
            and name instead of the URL to this file and most probably, =
when you=20
            deploy your client application, it's going to then need to =
find this=20
            WSDL file as well. </P>
            <P>The <CODE>Agent</CODE> property is another interesting =
property.=20
            If you look at the HTTP header that was shown earlier, =
you'll see=20
            that the <CODE>User-Agent</CODE> HTTP header was =
<CODE>Borland SOAP=20
            1.1</CODE>. Now you're free to change this to whatever else =
you'd=20
            like. I normally use the <CODE>User-Agent</CODE> HTTP header =
to=20
            distinguish my client applications from others. If you have =
to deal=20
            with security (proxies/firewalls), the =
<CODE>User-Agent</CODE> HTTP=20
            header can come in handy to allow for further filtering. For =

            example, you can tell you network security guys, to allow =
HTTP=20
            requests that have a <CODE>Content-Type</CODE> of text/xml=20
            <B>ONLY</B> if the <CODE>User-Agent</CODE> is xyz and the=20
            <CODE>SOAPAction</CODE> header is abc. Etc. </P>
            <P>The <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component can be lifetime =
managed as a=20
            component or as an Interfaced object. It is managed, as a =
component=20
            when its owner is not nil. If you drop it on a form, its =
owner is=20
            the form and so it is managed as a component. This becomes =
important=20
            to know in the following case: </P>
            <P>In the <CODE>OnClick</CODE> event of code listed above, =
you'll=20
            notice that we've not declared a variable of the type=20
            <CODE>IEmailService</CODE>. But what if we needed to do =
this? What=20
            if we needed a global variable of this type in our =
application?=20
            Well, we declare a variable of this type in the =
<CODE>private</CODE>=20
            section and use it in the <CODE>OnClick</CODE> event and =
elsewhere=20
            in our code. When we do this, we need to be sure to nil out =
this=20
            variable in the <CODE>destructor</CODE> of the form (or some =
other=20
            appropriate place). The code below shows and example of =
this. </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE><PRE><FONT face=3D"Courier =
New"><FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>type</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>TForm1</FONT> <FONT color=3D#ff0000>=3D</FONT> =
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>class</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>TForm</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#000080>Button1</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> =
<FONT color=3D#000080>TButton</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>procedure</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>Button1Click</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Sender</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>TObject</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>procedure</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>FormDestroy</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Sender</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>TObject</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>private</B></FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#000080>EmailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> <FONT color=3D#000080>IEmailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#808080>{ Private declarations }</FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>public</B></FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#808080>{ Public declarations }</FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>var</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>Form1</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>TForm1</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>implementation</B></FONT>

<FONT color=3D#808080>{$R *.dfm}</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>procedure</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>TForm1</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Button1Click</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>Sender</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>TObject</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>begin</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>EmailService</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>:=3D</FONT> <FONT color=3D#000080>HTTPRIO1</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#0000ff><B>as</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>IEmailService</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>procedure</B></FONT> <FONT =
