            <H2>Building the Send an EMail WebService Server</H2>
            <P>Before we begin with building the Send an Email =
Webservice, let=20
            me take you through the paces of what we will be doing. </P>
            <P>We're going to start a new SOAP Server Application using =
the=20
            Wizard. This essentially produces a Web server extension =
(CGI, ISAPI=20
            etc.) that contains a Web Module and some components on it. =
This is=20
            really very similar to a Web Broker application. We'll =
choose the=20
            ISAPI option. </P>
            <DIV class=3Dnote><IMG alt=3Dexclamation.gif src=3D"" =
border=3D0> If you're=20
            not familiar with ISAPI applications, I suggest you look at =
the=20
            tutorials on my web site related to ISAPI applications. The=20
            WebServices you'll build with the help of tutorials on this =
web site=20
            will mostly be ISAPI applications. Your knowledge of ISAPI=20
            Applications/debuggin etc. will be leveraged here. If you =
don't have=20
            experience building ISAPI applications, debugging etc. I =
strongly=20
            suggest you read the tutorials on my web site for this =
before you=20
            attempt to build WebServices. This way you'll have a firmer =
grasp of=20
            what is going on and this will save you (and me <G>) endless =
hours=20
            of frustration.=20
            <P>
            <P>I assume you know ISAPI application development and as a =
result,=20
            you will find that I do not explain ISAPI related concepts =
in any of=20
            the WebService tutorials. </P></G></DIV>
            <P>The standard SOAP Server application has three components =
on it.=20
            </P>
            <OL>
              <LI><CODE>THTTPSOAPDispatcher</CODE>=20
              <LI><CODE>THTTPSOAPPascalInvoker</CODE>=20
              <LI><CODE>TWSDLHTMLPublish</CODE> </LI></OL>
            <P>The <CODE>THTTPSOAPDispatcher</CODE> dispatches all =
requests that=20
            have <CODE>/soap</CODE> in the PathInfo. It then hands the =
request=20
            over to <CODE>THTTPSOAPPascalInvoker</CODE>. This component =
really=20
            handles the bulk of the process. What is this process? Well, =
If you=20
            take a look at the HTTP request header from earlier, you =
see, that=20
            the full request looks like this: </P><PRE><CODE>
http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/emailwebservice.dll/soap/IEmailservice
</CODE>
</PRE>
            <P>The PathInfo part of this request is: =
</P><PRE>/soap/IEmailservice

</PRE>
            <P>You learned earlier, that the =
<CODE>THTTPSOAPDispatcher</CODE>=20
            handles all requests, with <CODE>/soap</CODE> in it and =
hands it=20
            over to the <CODE>THTTPSOAPPascalInvoker</CODE>. So the=20
            <CODE>THTTPSOAPPascalInvoker</CODE> has to do something with =

            <CODE>/IEMailservice</CODE>. So what does it do? It looks up =
the=20
            Invocation Registry for any registered Interfaces that match =
the=20
            name IEMailservice. It also parses out the content of the =
web=20
            request (this is the SOAP Packet from earlier) and finds the =
name of=20
            the method and the parameters. Once it has done that, it=20
            instantiates an instance of the class that implements this =
interface=20
            and calls the required method handing it the parameters it =
received.=20
            Remember that it's able to do all this using RTTI for =
interfaces=20
            (new to Delphi 6) and some old RTTI magic. Since it receives =
all=20
            this information in the form of a string, it is converting =
these=20
            strings into method calls that instantiate an instance of a =
class=20
            and call the required methods (very much like a scripting =
engine).=20
            When you build a WebService, it is your job to: </P>
            <OL>
              <LI>Define an Interface.=20
              <LI>Implement this interface in a class.=20
              <LI>Register the interface and class with the invocation =
registry.=20
              </LI></OL>
            <P>That's it. The Delphi 6 SOAP framework handles the rest =
of it for=20
            you. What's the rest of it you might ask? </P>
            <OL>
              <LI>Parsing the SOAP envelope.=20
              <LI>Instantiating the required class.=20
              <LI>Calling the required method and passing the received =
(parsed)=20
              parameters to it=20
              <LI>Getting back a result from the method.=20
              <LI>Putting the result back in a SOAP response envelope =
and=20
              sending it back out.=20
              <LI>If there is an exception it constructs a SOAP Fault =
packet=20
              instead </LI></OL>
            <P>To put it in really simple terms! </P>
            <P>I hope this short explanation has cleared things up a =
bit. If it=20
            has, then we're ready to move on and start with building our =
first=20
            Delphi 6 WebService. </P>
            <H2>The SOAP Server Application</H2>
            <OL>
              <LI>From the Delphi main menu, choose File | New | Other=20
              <LI>Choose the WebServices tab and choose the SOAP server=20
              Application Expert=20
              <LI>In the next dialog, leave the ISAPI/NSAPI (this is the =

              default) Dynamic Link Library option selected and Click =
OK.=20
            </LI></OL>
