            unit as MainU.pas. </P>
            <DIV class=3Dnote><IMG alt=3Dexclamation.gif src=3D"" =
border=3D0> Note: I=20
            assume at this point that you know about building ISAPI =
Applications=20
            and debugging them etc. I keep stressing this because I =
don't want=20
            to be flooded with emails asking me questions about =
WebServices that=20
            really pertain to ISAPI applications. I believe the =
information=20
            available on my web site related to ISAPI is more than =
enough to=20
            help you get started with ISAPI applications and get to the =
point=20
            where you can now build WebServices. </DIV>
            <P>
            <H2>Defining the Interface</H2>
            <P>If you've registered Delphi 6, you should then have =
access to=20
            some of the power toys that will be made available to you. =
At the=20
            time of this writing, I believe they are not yet available. =
Anyhow,=20
            I'll use (one of these toys) the Invokable Wizard that will =
help in=20
            building the WebService. Don't worry, if you don't have this =
wizard.=20
            What it does is it creates two new units for you, one that =
defines=20
            the Interface and the other that defines a class that =
implements=20
            this interface. I'll show you the full units, so you can =
create them=20
            by hand if required. Why have the interface defined in one =
unit and=20
            the implementation in another? Technically, there is =
obviously no=20
            need for this. But if you're building a client for the =
WebService as=20
            well (and 99% of the time you will, only if it's to test the =

            WebService), you can include the unit that declares your =
interface=20
            in the client application's project as well. This will =
eliminate the=20
            need to import the WSDL file (one of the steps we went =
through while=20
            building the client). However, if you want to test your =
WebService=20
            completely, you're better off building the client as we did =
earlier.=20
            That is, treat the client as an independent part of your =
system, so=20
            you know that others attempting to use the WebService will =
be able=20
            to. </P>
            <P>The Invokable Wizard can be found in the <CODE>New =
Items</CODE>=20
            dialog (File | New | Other | WebServices) as shown in =
<B>Figure=20
            7</B> below. </P>
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService9.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG src=3D"" border=3D0></A> =
<BR><B>Figure=20
            7: Showing the Invokable Wizard</B> </P>
            <P>As Shown in <B>Figure 8</B> below, the Invokable Wizard =
presents=20
            you with another dialog.=20
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService10.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG src=3D"" border=3D0></A> =
<BR><B>Figure=20
            8: Showing the dialog the Invokable Wizard presents you =
with.</B>=20
            </P>
            <P>I've filled in the values as shown there. You'll notice =
that the=20
            <CODE>InvokableClass</CODE> field has a combo box. I've =
chosen=20
            <CODE>TInvokableClass</CODE>. The other option is=20
            <CODE>TInterfacedObject</CODE>. There is a subtle difference =
between=20
            these two. <CODE>TInvokableClass</CODE> is derived from=20
            <CODE>TInterfacedObject</CODE> so it implements =
QueryInterface,=20
            AddRef and ReleaseRef. But it also has a virtual =
constructor. The=20
            fact that it has a virtual constructor allows the compiler =
to create=20
            an instance of the class using RTTI. If you need to derive =
from=20
            <CODE>TInterfacedObject</CODE> for some reason, you'll have =
to=20
            implement one extra step in your code (Ill show you that =
later) and=20
            register the class differently (I'll show you that too). All =
this,=20
            so the compiler can instantiate an instance of your class on =
the fly=20
            (That is <CODE>THTTPSOAPPascalInvoker</CODE> can instantiate =
an=20
            instance as was described earlier). </P>
            <P>The two units thus created are shown below. The first one =
is the=20
            unit that declares the Interface - =
<CODE>IEmailService</CODE> and=20
            the next one declares the class that will implement this =
interface -=20
            <CODE>TEmailService</CODE>. </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>The unit that declares the=20
            <CODE>IEMailService</CODE> Interface=20
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE><PRE><FONT face=3D"Courier =
New"><FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>unit</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>EMailServiceIntf</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>interface</B></FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>uses</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>Types</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>XSBuiltIns</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>type</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>IEMailService</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>=3D</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#0000ff><B>interface</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>IInvokable</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT>
    <FONT color=3D#ff0000>[</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#008000>'{A987FE00-E596-4733-83BB-E75ACA5FF363}'</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>]</FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>implementation</B></FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>uses</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>InvokeRegistry</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>initialization</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>InvRegistry</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>RegisterInterface</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>TypeInfo</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>IEMailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#008000>''</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#008000>''</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT></FONT>
</PRE></CODE></DIV></DIV><PRE></PRE>
            <P></P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>The unit that declares the=20
            <CODE>TEMailService</CODE> class=20
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE><PRE><FONT face=3D"Courier =
New"><FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>unit</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>EMailServiceImpl</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>interface</B></FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>uses</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>EMailServiceIntf</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>InvokeRegistry</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>type</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>TEMailService</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>=3D</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#0000ff><B>class</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>TInvokableClass</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> =
<FONT color=3D#000080>IEMailService</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>implementation</B></FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>initialization</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>InvRegistry</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>RegisterInvokableClass</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>TEMailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT></FONT>
</PRE></CODE></DIV></DIV><PRE></PRE>
            <P>Make a note of the units in the uses clause of both these =
units=20
            and the <CODE><B>initialization</B></CODE> section as well. =
If=20
            you're building these units, by hand, I suggest you do so at =
this=20
            point looking at the above listings. </P>
            <P>To understand the "Registration" need, I suggest you look =
at the=20
            on-line help for more information. Notice also, that the =
unit that=20
            declares the Interface is very similar to the one that was =
generated=20
            for us using the <CODE>WebService Importer</CODE> wizard =
back when=20
            we started with building the client application. Minus the =
SendMail=20
            method and GUID obviously. </P>
            <P>Lets go ahead and define the SendMail method as shown in =
the=20
            listing below. </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE><PRE><FONT face=3D"Courier =
New"><FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>unit</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>EMailServiceIntf</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>interface</B></FONT>
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>uses</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>Types</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>XSBuiltIns</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>type</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>IEmailService</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>=3D</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#0000ff><B>interface</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>IInvokable</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#ff0000>[</FONT><FONT =
