            <P>Save these units using the names shown (these are the =
names=20
            generated for you by the Wizard if you used it). New to =
Delphi 6 is=20
            the ability to use Code Insight to fill in the methods of an =

            interface in the class that implements the interface. You =
can even=20
            multi-select the methods shown to get Code Insight to write =
them all=20
            out for you as they were declared in the Interface. Note =
that the=20
            units need to be saved for this to work. </P>
            <P>So switch to the unit that declares the class the =
implements the=20
            <CODE>IEMailService</CODE>, move you cursor under the "T" of =

            <CODE>TEMailService</CODE> and hit CTRL Space. You should =
see the=20
            SendMail method listed in the code completion drop down. =
Select it=20
            and hit the Enter key and you'll see that Delphi does the =
rest for=20
            you. Now do the class completion (CTRL SHIFT C) </P>
            <P>We're using Indy to do the sending of emails. You'll =
notice that=20
            Indy is now a part of Delphi 6. Those of you who have not =
used Indy=20
            yet, will obviously need to get to know how to use them. Our =

            implementation of sending an email is nothing special, =
except for=20
            the fact that all objects are being created on the fly. You =
will=20
            also need to add the required units to the uses clause of =
your=20
            units. The listing below shows the full unit for the=20
            <CODE>TEmailService</CODE> class. Remember, this is the =
class that=20
            implements the interface <CODE>IEmailService</CODE>. </P>
