<FONT color=3D#808080>{ try }</FONT>
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>initialization</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>InvRegistry</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>RegisterInvokableClass</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>TEmailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT></FONT>
</PRE></CODE></DIV></DIV><PRE></PRE>
            <P>Add these two units to your project and we're almost done =
now.=20
            Pretty simple so far huh? The last thing that's left is =
getting the=20
            service application to respond to SOAP calls and publish the =
WSDL=20
            file. Remember, without a WSDL file, your WebService is =
practically=20
            useless to anyone else. </P>
            <H2>TWSDLPublish</H2>
            <P>What we need to do is select the =
<CODE>TWSDLPublish</CODE>=20
            component on the WebModule and set its =
<CODE>AdminEnabled</CODE>=20
            property to true. Now run your application and using a =
browser,=20
            navigate to this URL=20
            =
<CODE>http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/EmailWebService.dll/wsdl</CODE>.=20
            Change the domain and path according to your setup. Just be =
sure you=20
            put the <CODE>/wsdl</CODE> as a path of the final URL. You =
should=20
            see a screen similar to that shown in Figure 9. </P>
            <P>
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService11.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG src=3D"" border=3D0></A> =
<BR><B>Figure=20
            9: Showing the WSDL Administration Page</B> </P>
            <P>If you had not set the <CODE>AdminEnabled</CODE> property =
to=20
            True, you wouldn't see the <CODE>Administrator</CODE> =
button. Click=20
            on the <CODE>WSDL for IEmailService</CODE> link. You should =
now see=20
            a WSDL file in your browser. Scroll to the end of the =
document. You=20
            should see that the <CODE><B>&lt;service&gt;</B></CODE> =
element is=20
            an <CODE>empty tag</CODE> (as per XML definition). If you =
look at=20
            the WSDL file at the URL specified at the start of this =
tutorial,=20
            you should notice that the =
<CODE><B>&lt;service&gt;</B></CODE>=20
            element had a child element called =
<CODE><B>&lt;port&gt;</B></CODE>=20
            and the <CODE><B>&lt;port&gt;</B></CODE> element had a=20
            <CODE><B>&lt;soap:address&gt;</B></CODE> element as a child =
like so:=20
            </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE><PRE>&lt;port =
name=3D"IEmailServicePort" binding=3D"IEmailServicebinding"&gt;
  &lt;soap:address =
location=3D"http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/EmailWebservice.dll/soap/IEm=
ailService" /&gt;
</PRE></CODE></DIV></DIV>
            <P>We need to define the <CODE>Port</CODE> for our service. =
We do=20
            that by clicking on the <CODE>Administrator</CODE> button =
and then=20
            on the <CODE>WSDL for IEMailService</CODE> link. You should =
see a=20
            screen that looks like that shown in <B>Figure 10</B>. </P>
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService12.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG src=3D"" border=3D0></A> =
<BR><B>Figure=20
            10: Showing the Admin Screen that allows for registering a =
Port for=20
            the WebService</B> </P>
            <P>In the <CODE>PortName</CODE> field enter=20
            <CODE>IEmailServicePort</CODE> and in the =
<CODE>Address</CODE> field=20
            enter=20
            =
<CODE>http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/EmailWebservice.dll/soap/IEMailser=
vice</CODE>.=20
            Change the domain name as per your set up. Click the=20
            <CODE>Add</CODE> button. You should see a screen that looks =
like=20
            that shown in <B>Figure 11</B>. </P>
            <P><A =
href=3D"http://www.matlus.com/images/FirstWebService13.jpg"=20
            target=3D_blank rel=3Dnofollow><IMG src=3D"" border=3D0></A> =
<BR><B>Figure=20
            11: Showing the Admin Screen that allows for registering a =
Port for=20
            the WebService</B> </P>
            <P>Hit the back button of your browser twice. This should =
show the=20
            URL=20
            =
<CODE>http://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/EmailWebService.dll/wsdl</CODE>=20
            in your browser. Now if you click on the <CODE>WSDL for=20
            IEMailService</CODE> link and check the WSDL file thus =
generated,=20
            the <CODE><B>&lt;service&gt;</B></CODE> and=20
            <CODE><B>&lt;port&gt;</B></CODE> elements are as they should =
be. You=20
            should note also, that this operation has generated an=20
            <CODE>.ini</CODE> file in the <CODE>/scripts</CODE> folder =
(the same=20
            folder in which your DLL is installed. This =
<CODE>.ini</CODE> file=20
            will have the same name as your ISAPI dll with the words=20
            <CODE>_WSDLADMIN.INI</CODE> appended to it. The contents of =
this=20
            file should be something like this: </P><PRE><CODE>
[IWSDLPublish]
IWSDLPublishPort=3Dhttp://matlus.matlus.com/scripts/EmailWebservice.dll/s=
oap/IWSDLPublish

[IEmailService]
IEmailServicePort=3Dhttp://matlus.matlus.com =
/scripts/EmailWebservice.dll/soap/IEmailService
</CODE>
</PRE>
            <P>When you deploy your WebService to your production =
server, you=20
            either need to go through these steps again, or simply copy =
this=20
            <CODE>.ini</CODE> file over to the production server and =
change the=20
            domain name parts in both sections to match that of your =
production=20
            server. Do not forget this step. Without this step, your =
WebService=20
            will be quite un-usable. </P>
            <H2>TInvokableClass or TInterfacedObject</H2>
            <P>Earlier, I mentioned that if you decide to use=20
            <CODE>TInterfacedObject</CODE> as the class to derive your=20
            implementation class, you'll need to do things differently. =
Here are=20
            the differences and why you need to do them.=20
            <CODE>TInterfacedObject</CODE> does not have a virtual =
constructor=20
            declared. As a result, there is no way for your application =
to=20
            instantiate an instance of your class at run time when it =
gets a=20
            SOAP request to do so. As usual, there is always an option =
with=20
            Delphi. (These guys think of everything). What you need to =
do is=20
            implement a "Factory" method for your class. This method =
needs to=20
            return an instance of your class as an =
<CODE><B>out</B></CODE>=20
            parameter. Further, for the run time to figure it out, you =
need to=20
            register this method when registering the class with the =
invocation=20
            registry so that the application can call this method to get =
back an=20
            instance of your class. This is how you should implement =
this method=20
            and register it. </P>
            <DIV class=3Dcodeblockouter>
            <DIV class=3DcodeblockInner><CODE><PRE><FONT face=3D"Courier =
New"><FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>procedure</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>EMailServiceFactory</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>out</B></FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>obj</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>:</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>TObject</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>begin</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>obj</FONT> <FONT color=3D#ff0000>:=3D</FONT> =
<FONT color=3D#000080>TEmailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>Create</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>
<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>initialization</B></FONT>
  <FONT color=3D#000080>InvRegistry</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#000080>RegisterInvokableClass</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>(</FONT><FONT color=3D#000080>TEMailService</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>,</FONT> <FONT =
color=3D#000080>EMailServiceFactory</FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>)</FONT><FONT color=3D#ff0000>;</FONT>

<FONT color=3D#0000ff><B>end</B></FONT><FONT =
color=3D#ff0000>.</FONT></FONT>
</PRE></CODE></DIV></DIV><PRE></PRE>
            <P>Note the change in the call to the=20
            <CODE>RegisterInvokableClass</CODE> method in the=20
            <CODE>initialization</CODE> section as well. </P>
            <P>Well, that pretty much does it. This tutorial contains =
more=20
            explanation than the actual work involved in building a =
WebService=20
            with Delphi 6. It's really very simple and I hope, after =
you've read=20
            this tutorial, you feel the same. Testing your WebService =
server is=20
            a simple task, now, since you already have a client. All you =
need to=20
            do is change the <CODE>WSDLLocation</CODE> property of the=20
            <CODE>THTTPRIO</CODE> component in your client application =
(or=20
            <CODE>URL</CODE> property as the case may be) to match that =
of your=20
            WebService (instead on mine). The projects are available for =

            download as a project group containing the WebService Client =
project=20
            and WebService server project. =
</P></SPAN></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
